{"id": "EUVD-2026-32762", "enisaUuid": "ef7bfb62-18ec-3606-a261-814903d03b31", "description": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnvmet-tcp: fix race between ICReq handling and queue teardown\n\nnvmet_tcp_handle_icreq() updates queue->state after sending an\nInitialization Connection Response (ICResp), but it does so without\nserializing against target-side queue teardown.\n\nIf an NVMe/TCP host sends an Initialization Connection Request\n(ICReq) and immediately closes the connection, target-side teardown\nmay start in softirq context before io_work drains the already\nbuffered ICReq. In that case, nvmet_tcp_schedule_release_queue()\nsets queue->state to NVMET_TCP_Q_DISCONNECTING and drops the queue\nreference under state_lock.\n\nIf io_work later processes that ICReq, nvmet_tcp_handle_icreq() can\nstill overwrite the state back to NVMET_TCP_Q_LIVE. That defeats the\nDISCONNECTING-state guard in nvmet_tcp_schedule_release_queue() and\nallows a later socket state change to re-enter teardown and issue a\nsecond kref_put() on an already released queue.\n\nThe ICResp send failure path has the same problem. If teardown has\nalready moved the queue to DISCONNECTING, a send error can still\noverwrite the state with NVMET_TCP_Q_FAILED, again reopening the\nwindow for a second teardown path to drop the queue reference.\n\nFix this by serializing both post-send state transitions with\nstate_lock and bailing out if teardown has already started.\n\nUse -ESHUTDOWN as an internal sentinel for that bail-out path rather\nthan propagating it as a transport error like -ECONNRESET. Keep\nnvmet_tcp_socket_error() setting rcv_state to NVMET_TCP_RECV_ERR before\nhonoring that sentinel so receive-side parsing stays quiesced until the\nexisting release path completes.", "datePublished": "2026-05-28T09:35:49", "dateUpdated": "2026-10-19T19:36:51", "baseScore": 9.8, "baseScoreVersion": "3.1", "baseScoreVector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H", "references": ["https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/b7dd4d27aa70bd98bb10572310e913668baf6a65", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/9c63cf80895a70eb4fcfcaa725bb1ac9ae76f02b", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/6f96dea4819d122737b217ea16660d255abbf8c6", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/5f0b95ef68ab9afba75b20eebf436130f80c161a", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/49891c8fe0cb43fbbe480da1cdccfbbaeb820cb3", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/67e1aaf93b495c2f10bc8a5fbba575fbb7f449b6", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/dcfe4d1f7960e7d1c01642318f3aae1a604f8508", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/5293a8882c549fab4a878bc76b0b6c951f980a61"], "aliases": ["GHSA-pp4w-65w8-f5f3", "CVE-2026-46135"], "assigner": "Linux", "epss": 0.4, "enisaIdProduct": [{"id": "02a11395-3c4a-37c0-b642-c3c445cdf224", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.12.88"}, {"id": "0554a1ee-c8c9-37f3-a46d-fa8bd3630c06", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "872d26a391da92ed8f0c0f5cb5fef428067b7f30 <67e1aaf93b495c2f10bc8a5fbba575fbb7f449b6"}, {"id": "3742382c-69b4-39f0-9f10-8bc2bf552b62", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "5.0"}, {"id": "40968b1c-2b5e-3700-8037-7381b5c30c2e", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "872d26a391da92ed8f0c0f5cb5fef428067b7f30 <b7dd4d27aa70bd98bb10572310e913668baf6a65"}, {"id": "43e44fe3-a67c-34fb-8e61-1754f7cabb08", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "872d26a391da92ed8f0c0f5cb5fef428067b7f30 <5293a8882c549fab4a878bc76b0b6c951f980a61"}, {"id": "56a9899c-0cb3-37f2-b73e-fd484561bfad", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 0"}, {"id": "58447e22-cfd8-372a-ae17-183957bc59ac", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "872d26a391da92ed8f0c0f5cb5fef428067b7f30 <49891c8fe0cb43fbbe480da1cdccfbbaeb820cb3"}, {"i